1
00:00:04,698 --> 00:00:08,359
Chapter 24

2
00:00:10,679 --> 00:00:15,658
It was now the eighth day since I'd had my accident in the desert,

3
00:00:15,658 --> 00:00:19,439
and I'd listen to the story of the merchant as I was drinking the last drop

4
00:00:19,439 --> 00:00:22,579
of my water supply

5
00:00:24,479 --> 00:00:30,479
I said to the little prince, "these memories of yours are very charming;

6
00:00:30,479 --> 00:00:37,618
but I have not yet succeeded in repairing my plane; I have nothing more to drink;

7
00:00:37,618 --> 00:00:41,819
and I, too, should be very happy if I could walk at my leisure toward a spring

8
00:00:41,819 --> 00:00:44,279
of fresh water!"

9
00:00:44,279 --> 00:00:48,300
"My friend the fox--" the little prince said to me.

10
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:55,020
"My dear little man, this is no longer a matter that has anything to do with the fox!"

11
00:00:55,020 --> 00:00:57,000
"Why not?"

12
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,779
"Because I'm about to die of thirst..."

13
00:01:00,779 --> 00:01:08,279
He did not follow my reasoning, and he answered me the thing to have a friend,

14
00:01:08,279 --> 00:01:10,500
even if one is about to die.

15
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:15,118
I, for instance I'm very glad to have a fox as a friend..."

16
00:01:15,118 --> 00:01:20,698
"He has no way of guessing the danger," I said to myself.

17
00:01:20,698 --> 00:01:24,719
"He has never been either very hungry or thirsty.

18
00:01:24,719 --> 00:01:29,158
A little sunshine is all that he needs..."

19
00:01:29,158 --> 00:01:36,779
But he looked at me steadily, and replied to my thought: "I am thirsty, too.

20
00:01:36,779 --> 00:01:39,479
Let us look for a well..."

21
00:01:39,479 --> 00:01:42,238
I made a gesture of weariness.

22
00:01:42,238 --> 00:01:48,179
It is absurd to look for a well, at random, in the immensity of the desert.

23
00:01:48,179 --> 00:01:53,359
But nevertheless we started walking.

24
00:01:54,179 --> 00:02:00,479
When we trudged along for several hours, in silence, the darkness fell,

25
00:02:00,479 --> 00:02:06,779
and the stars began to come out first it made me a little feverish,

26
00:02:06,779 --> 00:02:11,460
and I looked at them as if I were in a dream.

27
00:02:11,460 --> 00:02:19,380
The little prince's last words came reeling back into my memory: "Then you are thirsty, too?"

28
00:02:19,380 --> 00:02:21,420
I demanded.

29
00:02:21,420 --> 00:02:24,118
But he did not reply to my question.

30
00:02:24,118 --> 00:02:31,139
He merely said to me: "Water may also be good for the heart..."

31
00:02:31,139 --> 00:02:36,359
I did not understand this answer, but I said nothing.

32
00:02:36,359 --> 00:02:40,859
I knew very well that it was impossible to cross-examine him.

33
00:02:40,859 --> 00:02:42,840
He was tired.

34
00:02:42,840 --> 00:02:44,880
He sat down.

35
00:02:44,880 --> 00:02:47,399
I sat down beside him.

36
00:02:47,399 --> 00:02:52,760
And, after a little silence, he spoke again:

37
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:59,939
"The stars are beautiful, because of a flower that cannot be seen."

38
00:02:59,939 --> 00:03:05,760
I replied, "Yes, that is so."

39
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:10,439
And, without saying anything more, I looked across the ridges of sand that

40
00:03:10,439 --> 00:03:14,340
were stretched out before us in the moonlight.

41
00:03:14,340 --> 00:03:20,219
"The desert looks so beautiful," the little prince added.

42
00:03:20,219 --> 00:03:25,260
And that was true I've always loved the desert.

43
00:03:25,260 --> 00:03:30,840
One sits down on a desert sand dune, sees nothing here's nothing.

44
00:03:30,840 --> 00:03:37,579
Yet through the silence something throbs, and gleams...

45
00:03:38,698 --> 00:03:47,899
"What makes the desert beautiful," said the little prince in his reverie is that

46
00:03:47,899 --> 00:03:52,579
somewhere it hides so well..."

47
00:03:52,800 --> 00:04:00,359
I was astonished by a sudden understanding of that mysterious radiation of the sands.

48
00:04:00,359 --> 00:04:04,800
When I was a little boy I lived in an old house, and legend told us that a

49
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:06,779
treasure was buried there.

50
00:04:06,779 --> 00:04:11,279
To be sure, no one had ever known how to find it; perhaps no one had ever even

51
00:04:11,279 --> 00:04:16,439
looked for it complete cast an enchantment over that house.

52
00:04:16,439 --> 00:04:22,819
My home was hiding a secret in the depths of its heart

53
00:04:24,359 --> 00:04:27,118
I said the little prince.

54
00:04:27,118 --> 00:04:36,380
"The house, the stars, the desert-- what gives them their Beauties something that is invisible!"

55
00:04:36,380 --> 00:04:44,000
"I am glad," he said, "that you agree with my fox

56
00:04:44,880 --> 00:04:50,579
was the little prince dropped off to sleep, I took him in my arms and set out

57
00:04:50,579 --> 00:04:53,340
walking once more.

58
00:04:53,340 --> 00:04:57,000
I felt deeply moved, and stirred.

59
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:01,618
It seemed to me that I was carrying a very fragile treasure.

60
00:05:01,618 --> 00:05:07,679
It seemed to me, even, that there was nothing more fragile on all the Earth.

61
00:05:07,679 --> 00:05:13,260
In the moonlight I looked at his pale forehead, his closed eyes, his locks of

62
00:05:13,260 --> 00:05:18,719
hair that trembled in the wind, and I said to myself:

63
00:05:18,719 --> 00:05:23,219
"What I see here is nothing but a shell.

64
00:05:23,219 --> 00:05:28,439
What is most important is invisible..."

65
00:05:28,439 --> 00:05:36,179
As his lips open slightly with the suspicion of a half smile I said to myself, again:

66
00:05:36,179 --> 00:05:41,158
"What moves me so deeply, about this little prince who is sleeping here,

67
00:05:41,158 --> 00:05:46,380
is his loyalty to a flower-- the image of a rose that shines through

68
00:05:46,380 --> 00:05:53,539
his whole being like the flame of a lamp, even when he is asleep..."

69
00:05:54,420 --> 00:05:59,399
And I felt him to be more fragile still.

70
00:05:59,399 --> 00:06:04,859
I felt the need of protecting him, as if he himself were a flame that might

71
00:06:04,859 --> 00:06:10,099
be extinguished by a little puff of wind...

72
00:06:10,679 --> 00:06:21,380
And, as I walked on so, I found the well, at daybreak
